pageType: content
background: { type: solid, color: "$paper" }
notes: |
  Slide 6: Sky Localization & Triangulation: Pointing Telescopes in the Sky.
  - Explains the geometrical principles of gravitational-wave sky localization: time-of-flight differences between detector pairs constrain the source direction to an annulus on the celestial sphere.
  - Compares 2-detector baseline vs 3-detector and 4-detector networks: 2 detectors (Hanford-Livingston) produce degenerate arcs of ~600 deg^2 (like GW150914); adding Virgo narrows the region to ~28 deg^2 (like GW170817), while adding KAGRA pinpoints targets to ~10 deg^2.
  - Discusses pipeline latencies: BAYESTAR produces rapid HEALPix probability maps in under 5 seconds for robotic telescope pointing, while full coherent Bayesian Markov Chain Monte Carlo (LALInference / Bilby) runs offline.
